abstract: >-
  Follow-up study to "The Borrow Checker as a Binary Auditor" (DOI: 10.5281/zenodo.24261883)
  introducing Stage 1.5 Multi-Edge Reachability and Linker-Hygiene Auditing across 53 stripped
  AArch64 ELF binaries (28 Samsung Galaxy S9 baseline binaries, 7 Galaxy S25 Ultra Android 16
  binaries, and 18 Galaxy S26 Ultra Android 16 binaries plus a signed Biometric Face APEX).
  Demonstrates why naive ELF DT_NEEDED/.dynsym scanners fail on mobile firmware due to four
  dynamic linkage mechanisms (dlopen plugin tables, JNI RegisterNatives, .data.rel.ro vtables,
  and linker namespaces) and five C++ ABI collision traps. Classifies all 20 previously
  discovered C/C++ defects (SAM-BUG-01 through SAM-BUG-20) into Class I (Live Wired Production
  Paths: 16/20), Class II (Intra-Binary Dead Fossils & Cloned Inlines), and Class III
  (Cross-Generation Zombie Activation, exemplified by SAM-BUG-15 transitioning from an uncalled
  2018 S9 export to a live 2026 S26 Ultra fastJpegDecode callsite). Quantifies pervasive
  -fvisibility=hidden omissions (90%-95% GLOBAL DEFAULT export bloat and 100% PLT interposition
  in 16 of 18 S26 Ultra libraries) and includes the standalone #![forbid(unsafe_code)] Safe Rust
  companion crate s9_blur_detection verified across 30,118 SplitMix64 + boundary FFI vectors (0 ULP,
  1.18x-2.66x speedup).
